WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:15.460
Secure gRPC services need observability across service calls, authentication decisions, authorization denials, latency, deadlines, cancellations, rejected messages, streaming behavior, and dependency failures.

2
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:21.560
These signals help teams understand whether the service is behaving as intended and whether resource controls are working.

3
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:31.280
Logs should include useful security and operational signals without recording tokens, secrets, raw sensitive payloads, or excessive personal data.

4
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:41.540
Useful logs identify the service, method, outcome category, correlation identifier, latency, deadline behavior, and policy result where appropriate.

5
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.720
They should not turn protected service traffic into a second sensitive database.

6
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.640
Reflection can help tooling discover service contracts, but production exposure should be intentional and environment-scoped.

7
00:00:52.740 --> 00:01:00.740
Teams should know whether reflection is enabled, who can reach it, what it reveals, and how that aligns with documentation and governance expectations.

8
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:06.860
JSON transcoding can expose HTTP and JSON endpoints from gRPC services.

9
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.840
That can be valuable for integration, but it creates a web API surface that needs review.

10
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:25.460
Authentication, authorization, validation, error behavior, logging, CORS, documentation, and production exposure should be reviewed just as they would be for any other API.

11
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.420
Infrastructure can affect gRPC security and reliability.

12
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:45.520
Reverse proxies, load balancers, service meshes, ingress controllers, gateways, and hosting platforms can influence TLS, HTTP/2, headers, deadlines, message size, connection behavior, and logging.

13
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.220
Development settings should not silently become production behavior.

14
00:01:49.500 --> 00:02:01.580
Production readiness includes secrets management, certificate rotation, dependency review, deployment configuration, monitoring, incident handoff, and documented exceptions.

15
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.200
A secure gRPC service is not only well-coded.

16
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:09.700
It is also operable, observable, and owned after release.
